WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:04.200
What if chat could change the rules while you play?

00:00:04.833 --> 00:00:06.833
This is LIVEFORGE.

00:00:06.833 --> 00:00:09.400
I'm carrying an ember through The Last Light.

00:00:09.567 --> 00:00:11.633
There's a broken crossing ahead.

00:00:11.967 --> 00:00:14.600
Let's add a floating wedge that glides back

00:00:14.600 --> 00:00:15.800
and forth over the gap.

00:00:17.300 --> 00:00:20.433
Here, I send that idea in Twitch with

00:00:20.433 --> 00:00:22.333
exclamation mark forge.

00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:25.067
It arrives in the studio with my name,

00:00:25.133 --> 00:00:27.367
and another chat command adds support.

00:00:28.067 --> 00:00:29.767
I choose the proposal.

00:00:29.900 --> 00:00:32.400
The audience suggests what happens next;

00:00:32.633 --> 00:00:34.467
I decide what reaches the game.

00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:40.533
Forge sends the idea to the Codex SDK

00:00:40.533 --> 00:00:42.700
in an isolated sandbox.

00:00:42.867 --> 00:00:45.433
It writes a new rule from the world's

00:00:45.433 --> 00:00:47.733
physics, platforms, and motion.

00:00:48.233 --> 00:00:50.800
The game stays playable while that runs.

00:00:51.133 --> 00:00:53.300
We keep the actual source,

00:00:53.300 --> 00:00:56.100
generation record, and verification results.

00:00:57.800 --> 00:01:01.400
Now I open the preview. Watch this:

00:01:01.633 --> 00:01:04.533
the wedge slides across the gap,

00:01:04.600 --> 00:01:06.833
never quite reaching either side,

00:01:06.933 --> 00:01:09.000
so I have to time my jumps.

00:01:09.400 --> 00:01:13.167
I can test the change before approving this exact version.

00:01:13.833 --> 00:01:16.467
The verifier checks bounded behavior and

00:01:16.467 --> 00:01:20.400
recovery, while I decide whether the mechanic feels right.

00:01:20.867 --> 00:01:22.867
The live world pauses here,

00:01:22.867 --> 00:01:24.400
so I can take my time.

00:01:29.133 --> 00:01:30.500
I approve it,

00:01:30.500 --> 00:01:32.400
then activate it at a checkpoint.

00:01:32.900 --> 00:01:36.000
The same wedge appears in the live world.

00:01:36.400 --> 00:01:37.700
I wait for it,

00:01:37.700 --> 00:01:40.000
jump on, ride it across,

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:41.433
then leap to the far side.

00:01:42.233 --> 00:01:45.200
Chat's idea has become part of this run.

00:01:45.533 --> 00:01:48.333
The author's name stays attached to the law.

00:01:51.100 --> 00:01:53.967
The streamer keeps control throughout.

00:01:54.233 --> 00:01:55.967
I can close intake,

00:01:56.133 --> 00:01:58.300
hide an unsuitable idea,

00:01:58.467 --> 00:02:01.667
or restore the previous law and checkpoint.

00:02:02.067 --> 00:02:03.333
Here I restore.

00:02:04.100 --> 00:02:05.567
The wedge is gone,

00:02:05.567 --> 00:02:09.167
and we're back at the saved moment before activation.

00:02:09.700 --> 00:02:11.467
Nothing gets approved automatically.

00:02:13.067 --> 00:02:16.033
We built LIVEFORGE with Codex too.

00:02:16.367 --> 00:02:18.933
This is the actual build conversation,

00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:21.333
including a repair made during testing.

00:02:22.033 --> 00:02:25.400
A short keypress once disappeared between game ticks.

00:02:25.633 --> 00:02:28.267
The fix keeps that input until the next

00:02:28.267 --> 00:02:31.800
tick, with a regression test showing the crank opens once.

00:02:33.500 --> 00:02:36.533
The public overlay keeps the current idea and

00:02:36.533 --> 00:02:39.033
its author visible for the audience.

00:02:39.433 --> 00:02:42.533
Creators can also try a clearly labeled sample

00:02:42.533 --> 00:02:43.667
without signing in.

00:02:44.767 --> 00:02:48.200
LIVEFORGE gives chat a creative role inside the

00:02:48.200 --> 00:02:51.500
adventure, with the streamer still directing the show.
